From b3c484484334a301a68c1544ee202f0404c6e3e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 13 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libxl: write online=0 in the QEMU bodge path so "xl devd"
 reaps the driver-domain device model on domain destroy

Disaggregated setup: the toolstack (dom0) creates the guest with virtio
backends in a driver domain (backend_domid != 0); the driver domain runs
"xl devd", which spawns the qemu device model for the guest (QDISK/QVIRTIO
add_device -> libxl__spawn_qdisk_backend) and is the only entity that can
kill it again (remove_device -> libxl__destroy_qdisk_backend; the pid only
exists in the driver domain's tree, libxl/<domid>/qdisk-backend-pid).

On "xl destroy" the device removal protocol between the two sides is:

 toolstack: write online=0 + state=Closing, wait for state==Closed
            (libxl__initiate_device_generic_remove), then wait for the
            *driver domain* to remove the backend dir (device_hotplug ->
            device_destroy_be_watch_cb);
 devd:      on state==XenbusStateClosed && online==0
            (backend_watch_callback) -> remove_device -> kill qemu via
            libxl__destroy_qdisk_backend() when the guest's last
            QDISK/QVIRTIO device goes away + libxl__device_destroy()
            removes the backend dir, which unblocks the toolstack.

This protocol is broken for QEMU-owned backends on a dying/paused domain
(always the case for "xl destroy", which pauses the guest first and runs
the removal with LIBXL__FORCE_ON): libxl__initiate_device_generic_remove()
takes the "TODO: 4.2 Bodge due to QEMU" branch, which returns early and
*skips* the transaction that clears online; the 2s bodge timeout handler
device_qemu_timeout() then only forces state=6 (Closed) and leaves
online==1.  devd's removal condition (state == Closed && online == 0) is
therefore never true, so devd neither kills the qemu it spawned nor
removes the backend nodes.  The toolstack then logs, per device:

  libxl_device.c:1358:device_destroy_be_watch_cb: Domain <N>: timed out
  while waiting for /local/domain/<be>/backend/qvirtio/<N>/<dev> to be
  removed

and libxl__devices_destroy fails; the stale qemu in the driver domain
keeps the guest's block devices open and its grant/event-channel
references alive, leaving a zombie "(null)" domain until qemu is killed
and the backend/device-model nodes are removed by hand.

QVIRTIO (xen-troops) is part of QEMU_BACKEND(), so every virtio device of
the guest hits this path - observed on RPi5 (DomD = dom0less driver
domain, 7x qvirtio backend, timeout x7 on every destroy).

Fix: when device_qemu_timeout() force-closes the backend, also write
online=0 (before the state write, and only while the backend dir still
exists so we do not re-create it after the driver domain already cleaned
it up).  This makes devd's existing reaping machinery fire exactly as in
the non-bodge path: qemu is SIGHUPed, the backend dir is removed, the
toolstack's backend-removal watch completes, and the domain is fully
destroyed.  For the local (non driver domain) case writing online=0 is
what the regular removal transaction would have done anyway and is
harmless: the node is removed right afterwards by device_hotplug_done ->
libxl__device_destroy.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-4-8
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 tools/libs/light/libxl_device.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

--- a/tools/libs/light/libxl_device.c
+++ b/tools/libs/light/libxl_device.c
@@ -1099,6 +1099,7 @@
     STATE_AO_GC(aodev->ao);
     char *be_path = libxl__device_backend_path(gc, aodev->dev);
     char *state_path = GCSPRINTF("%s/state", be_path);
+    char *online_path = GCSPRINTF("%s/online", be_path);
     const char *xs_state;
     xs_transaction_t t = 0;
 
@@ -1123,6 +1124,28 @@
-        rc = libxl__xs_read_checked(gc, XBT_NULL, state_path, &xs_state);
+        rc = libxl__xs_read_checked(gc, t, state_path, &xs_state);
         if (rc) goto out;
 
+        /*
+         * Also force the backend offline.  When the backend lives in a
+         * driver domain, "xl devd" (backend_watch_callback/remove_device)
+         * only reaps the device - and, for QDISK/QVIRTIO backends, the
+         * qemu device model it spawned for the guest - when it observes
+         * state == XenbusStateClosed *and* online == 0.  This bodge path
+         * skipped the regular Closing transaction in
+         * libxl__initiate_device_generic_remove() where online is
+         * normally cleared, so without this write the driver domain
+         * never kills the device model nor removes the backend nodes:
+         * the toolstack then times out in device_destroy_be_watch_cb()
+         * waiting for the backend path to disappear, and the stale qemu
+         * keeps the guest's disks, grants and event channels referenced
+         * (zombie domain).  Only write while the backend dir still
+         * exists, or we would re-create it behind the driver domain's
+         * back.
+         */
+        if ( xs_state ) {
+            rc = libxl__xs_write_checked(gc, t, online_path, "0");
+            if ( rc ) goto out;
+        }
+
         if (xs_state && atoi(xs_state) != XenbusStateClosed) {
-            rc = libxl__xs_write_checked(gc, XBT_NULL, state_path, GCSPRINTF("%d", XenbusStateClosed));
+            rc = libxl__xs_write_checked(gc, t, state_path, GCSPRINTF("%d", XenbusStateClosed));
             if (rc) goto out;
